.workbuddy-ai/

# Other agents' folders and deploy archives: a tool drops these into the working
# tree and they are never repository content. `tests/test_repository_hygiene.py`
# asks git itself, and asserts that no rule here reaches a file the repository
# carries -- none is under `.claude/`, `.codebuddy/` or `.cursor/`, and no
# tracked file ends in `.zip` except the three the product site ships. Those
# three are named rather than added with `-f`: the Pyodide distribution's own
# `python_stdlib.zip`, which the try-it page's runtime loads -- as her source
# under `ui/site/static/` and as the copy `ui/site/build.py` publishes under
# `ui/dist/` -- and the bundle `ui/site/build.py` builds for the try-it page
# from this repository's files. `tests/test_site.py` holds the published set to
# being tracked, so an ignore rule cannot hide a published file again.
.claude/
.codebuddy/
.cursor/
*.zip
!ui/site/static/try/pyodide/python_stdlib.zip
!ui/dist/try/pyodide/python_stdlib.zip
!ui/dist/try/tribunal-core.zip

# Documents that arrive by hand rather than by a build: an exported PDF, a Word
# document, a slide deck, a file named after a CV. Matched case-insensitively --
# the bracket expressions, not `*.pdf` -- because the name is whatever the tool
# that wrote it chose, and gitignore is case-sensitive where the filesystem is.
# `tests/test_repository_hygiene.py` asks git itself, in both casings, and
# asserts that none of these rules reaches a file the repository carries.
*.[Pp][Dd][Ff]
*.[Dd][Oo][Cc][Xx]
*.[Pp][Pp][Tt][Xx]
*[Rr][Ee][Ss][Uu][Mm][Ee]*
